Description: PartitionAlloc's page allocator asks for JIT memory (MAP_JIT) for V8's code on Jam OS
 Jam OS keeps memory never both writable and executable, but in JIT
 memory: a mapping mmap makes with MAP_JIT (macOS's flag name, Jam OS's own
 value, in its <sys/mman.h>) in a process holding the JIT power (the
 program's `right jit`, docs/CR-PLAN.md section 12). Linux lets any
 anonymous memory become executable, so nothing on its path says which
 reservation will hold code, and a layer can't tell V8's code range from
 PartitionAlloc's own pools (making every reservation JIT memory would
 also keep it out of a forked child, which gets no writable JIT mapping).
 V8 says it already: its code range and its WebAssembly code space are
 reserved kNoAccessWillJitLater, which gin passes to PartitionAlloc as
 kInaccessibleWillJitLater, and PartitionAlloc turns into MAP_JIT on
 macOS. This hunk does the same on Jam OS. Decommits need nothing: Jam OS's
 mmap keeps a MAP_FIXED of private anonymous memory over JIT memory JIT
 memory (Linux's decommit by remap, DecommitAndZeroSystemPages).
Forwarded: not-needed (Jam OS's: its W^X rule)
---
--- a/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_posix.h
+++ b/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_posix.h
@@ -94,6 +94,19 @@
   }
 #endif
 
+#if defined(__jamos__)
+  // Jam OS (docs/CR-PLAN.md section 12) lets a program make memory
+  // executable only in JIT memory, which mmap makes when told so with
+  // MAP_JIT (macOS's flag, Jam OS's own value) in a process holding the JIT
+  // power. Linux lets any anonymous memory become executable, so its path
+  // never says which reservation will hold code; V8's code range and its
+  // WebAssembly code space say it here, as on macOS.
+  if (accessibility.permissions ==
+      PageAccessibilityConfiguration::kInaccessibleWillJitLater) {
+    map_flags |= MAP_JIT;
+  }
+#endif
+
   void* ret = mmap(reinterpret_cast<void*>(hint), length, access_flag,
                    map_flags, fd, 0);
   if (ret == MAP_FAILED) {
